#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572271} :activity/id #uuid "9c47e8cc-ad7c-5b83-9c9a-e2bee08c10d0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048388 :topic/id #uuid "9b5fc10c-ada7-55cd-8d40-6c4729395195" :topic/math-academy-id 3693} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572216 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c1d6a8-802b-5aac-9cd8-94b55465e8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495515} {:answer/id #uuid "382ad457-c78c-5db2-b511-049b1e51f672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{17}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495644} {:answer/id #uuid "95d62877-62ed-576b-b8c1-68b72d5fa0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495844} {:answer/id #uuid "dfda9d6c-2aba-514b-b5cf-e34408029568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496007} {:answer/id #uuid "f81daac3-e6be-5bee-9268-19f1a228f0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{22}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496007} :answer-field/id #uuid "10849e2a-d2b8-5eda-a82b-458c2096cbcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8f6a9cc3-415e-5254-bb6f-82cbdb814536" :question/math-academy-id "q-163566" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(4-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $1\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/73da2be2514373e6d0b1052f0eca21c339b4d826d38621faf72b3e251d7d8a95.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(4-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(4-2x) & =0 \\\\ 4-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2k>0$ | $0$ | $-2k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(4-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(4(0)-(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =k(4(1)-{(1)}^{2})+8 \\\\ 32 & =3k+8 \\\\ 3k & =24 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =32x-8{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-16{x}^{2}+64x+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(2)=4\\sqrt{-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1}=4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2cffda36-0576-57f3-9167-08b4838d2180" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75f9bb39-d662-5b7f-b26c-4c6a27fffc10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365290} {:answer/id #uuid "40369435-8306-5137-8429-a26ad39afe91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365291} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ede10b-ba4c-5e79-b731-b8038347c020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365292} {:answer/id #uuid "5a09cbc5-7125-5f56-932d-0251a7308a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365293} {:answer/id #uuid "07428674-2986-5965-9daa-705a8555f7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365290} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e05aabc-648f-50bd-bc69-595d4091baa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ecee2124-1bd8-5650-8e8b-4c51642487c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163553" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(6-6x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/61/61388466bb2d97b5080707e769ae862e633bfb0fe1d2b5f16c75daa14da76e53.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(6-6x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6-6x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-6x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $3>0$ | $0$ | $-6<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6-6x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (6-6x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=6x-3{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-3{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =6(0)-3(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-3{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =12x-6{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+12x+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+12x+8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(1)=\\sqrt{-6(1)^{2}+12(1)+8}=\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8bcf8cd4-92a9-5914-84aa-0e48430a0339" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572216}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572255 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2011be0c-38a3-5056-adae-7b634e1eedc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571594} {:answer/id #uuid "28c22842-c3ce-5a8a-ab8f-72781a08e807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571608} {:answer/id #uuid "31676b05-5ecf-5507-afe8-70e418952d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571621} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa15e1a-d450-5bdf-ab2e-7db7463df9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571758} {:answer/id #uuid "b0866692-8ed1-5f90-8e94-da9ca9c8496c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571758} :answer-field/id #uuid "07559b22-9501-5406-b1d4-af6af28cc58e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba2fee1b-3766-585f-9ea4-855819a270ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-163546" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of the acceleration is $\\frac{k}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $({e}^{4}-1)\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e9/e938c4ba0e20c0714d1cd0ef62fb6596b37341b06d29de4e675981007610e12a.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{k}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{k}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-\\frac{k}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=8-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=({e}^{4}-1)\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({e}^{4}-1)+1) \\\\ k\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4}) & =8 \\\\ 4k & =8 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4be8856-166e-5b28-9384-7b08fc3db5da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2269031-3808-5832-884f-e8b8017c9c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{4}{3}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365359} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6c3024-fa5b-5f46-969f-814b049ab789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{15}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365360} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcd61a1-599b-5b2c-85f2-4b2c81af9b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\pm 4\\sqrt{5}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365361} {:answer/id #uuid "67d1e270-938d-5f31-9382-769f0bc70e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\sqrt{5}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365362} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3ceed1-cddd-5143-b1a9-6164a52295df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{15}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365360} :answer-field/id #uuid "657f8a9e-940d-51df-b4a4-eb87c6fcb879" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7b27beee-357c-5af3-8233-0a884c5b276c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163574" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $6x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $4\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\,m,$ find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/fa/fa329a81ac533c3ab4bfd9ca6fb5b2ef81ff4e29fb8ff7f030c5e04952d9448d.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $6x\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-6x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-6x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-3{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-3{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =-3(2)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =8+12 \\\\ C & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=20-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =20-3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =20-3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{15}\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c8464206-7b26-5ba5-b25a-82075e1f6a55"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3d3836-b51f-5e7c-a7a9-704639b3e9bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495608} {:answer/id #uuid "528dca58-912a-5c4e-81e7-257ba6b0db7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495698} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4182f5-2803-552b-853e-c778c967ac69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495746} {:answer/id #uuid "c37c19ae-e778-56f2-937e-0465119bfe29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495938} {:answer/id #uuid "c8690093-4909-568c-bd3a-20b77037a0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495938} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a78871b-3da5-52c1-a57c-cb87914fafa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f22631e-a7c9-5925-8b08-e1ce77a0788a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163499" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k{x}^{2}(x-5)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $6\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/9a/9ac139d8823f326f32869f308cb2d04cdc9e0d86dba7d4a11b66955afd665da3.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k{x}^{2}(x-5)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{3})^{2} & =k(\\frac{(0)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(0)^{3}}{3})+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=6\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =k(\\frac{(6)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(6)^{3}}{3})+6 \\\\ 0 & =k(324-360)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-36k+6 \\\\ 36k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d8c82ddc-f5d7-56cd-8ad4-d3431241051f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572255}}] :activity/title "Determining Properties of Objects Described as Functions of Displacement" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571529}]}
